<h2>The case and the conviction</h2>
<p>The victim, a boy of about eight and a half by school records, described penetrative assaults by the appellant on two occasions about eight days apart in a room near a staircase at Rishi Nagar, with a threat to kill his mother. The matter surfaced when his uncle noticed bleeding. The trial court — the Fast Track Special Court (POCSO), Saket — convicted the appellant on 30 November 2023 and, on 6 March 2024, imposed 25 years&rsquo; RI under Section 6 POCSO with a Rs. 10,000 fine, 18 months under Section 506 IPC, and Rs. 7,50,000 compensation. On appeal, the defence attacked the related-witness character of the evidence, inconsistencies in the disclosure narrative, possible tutoring and uncalled witnesses. The High Court held the discrepancies <em>&ldquo;do not create a reasonable doubt&rdquo;</em> and affirmed the conviction, noting it is settled <em>&ldquo;that the testimony of a child victim may, by itself, form the basis of conviction if it inspires confidence.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>The sentencing error, and its correction</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Law on the date of offence (February 2019)</h3>
    <p>Section 6 POCSO: rigorous imprisonment of not less than 10 years, extendable to imprisonment for life, with fine.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Law after 16 August 2019 (Act 25 of 2019)</h3>
    <p>Section 6 POCSO: minimum raised to 20 years, extendable to imprisonment for the remainder of natural life, with fine; death in aggravated cases.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The trial court&rsquo;s 25-year term was fixed within the amended range. The High Court, quoting <strong>Sonu Kushwaha</strong> — <em>&ldquo;the amended provision will not apply to this case as the incident has taken place prior to 16-8-2019&rdquo;</em> — re-sentenced within the unamended range. Weighing the appellant&rsquo;s age of about 22 at the time, absence of similar antecedents and custody since 6 March 2019 against the victim&rsquo;s age and the repeated assaults with threats, it held that ten years&rsquo; RI <em>&ldquo;would adequately meet the ends of justice&rdquo;</em> and does not amount to retrospective application of the amended minimum. The appeal was partly allowed to that limited extent.</p>

<h2>The wider point for POCSO appeals</h2>
<p>Offences tried years after commission straddle the 2019 amendment with some frequency, and sentencing errors of this kind survive into appeal because the conviction debate absorbs all attention. The applicable checklist is short: fix the date of the offence; identify the text of the punishing provision on that date; and test the sentence against that text, not the one in force at trial. Where the trial court has applied the amended minimum to a pre-amendment offence, the error is one of law, correctable in appeal without disturbing the conviction — precisely the course taken here.</p>
